# 1. xArm Python SDK Installation
## 1.1 Installing using pip
```
pip install xarm-python-sdk
```

## 1.2 Installing by cloning the Github
### Step 1
Find the GitHub link for [xArm-Python-SDK](https://github.com/xArm-Developer/xArm-Python-SDK)

### Step 2
```
git clone https://github.com/xArm-Developer/xArm-Python-SDK.git
```
### Step 3
After cloning then run the commands in terminal 
- Windows
```powershell
python setup.py install
 ```
- Linux (or mac)
```terminal
python3 setup.py install 
```

# 2. Wiring the Robot Arm
Follow the instruction in the package to set up the robot.

### 2.1 Connecting multiple robots via Lan
Materials:
- Router
- ethernet cables
- Windows settings configuration (Network > ethernet settings)


When connecting several robots to one computer, often they will have different IP addresses
For example: 
- Opentron Flex: 
    - IP Address: 169.254.29.164 (Direct connection)
    - Subnet mask: 255.255.0.0
    - Port: 31950 
    - Jupyter port: 48888
- Ufactory xArm5
    - IP Address: 192.168.1.xxx
    - Port: :18333

By connecting it through a router, we can controll the IP's and requests that get sent to each robot!

# 3. Connecting the robot in python or Jupyter notebook
Run the following code in a cell or in a script to connect to the robot.
(Also can be tested in terminal )

``` Python 
from xarm.wrapper import XArmAPI
arm = XArmAPI('192.168.1.217')
arm.connect()
```
It will return 

> ROBOT_IP: 192.168.1.217, VERSION: v2.7.1, PROTOCOL: V1, DETAIL: 5,5,XF1305,AC1304,v2.7.1, TYPE1300: [1, 1]
change protocol identifier to 3



To disconnect run:
```python
arm.disconnect()
```
It will return a '0' if succesful 


